### Post Quantum IAB Workshop Update
*Presented by Nick Sullivan*  
*Slide Deck:* [02_Post Quantum IAB Workshop](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-iabopen-pqws-00)

* **Overview:** The workshop was held October 11–12 in Prague, chaired by Nick Sullivan and Jaroslav Holý, with ~40 attendees and 22 accepted position papers.
* **Technical Challenges:**
  * Underlying NIST cryptographic standards (ML-DSA, SLH-DSA) remain sound, but post-quantum (PQ) signatures introduce significant size increases (kilobytes vs. 64 bytes), impacting protocol performance (e.g., DTLS 1.2 degradation).
  * Root of trust updates present long deployment timelines (e.g., 20–30 year hardware lifetimes for HSMs/smartcards; package registries like NPM requiring long lead times).
  * David Benjamin noted that systems remain vulnerable to quantum attacks until non-PQ roots of trust are deprecated.
* **Proposed Mitigations:**
  * **Merkle Tree Certificates:** Explored in the LAMPS Working Group to amortize signature and public key distribution sizes.
  * **Alternative Paradigms:** Use of HMAC for JSON Web Tokens (Netflix) to trade off communication size against key distribution; KEM-based authentication designs (previously used in IPsec).

