### Invited Talk: Delegated Authorization for AI Agents
*Presented by Daza Greenwood*  
*Slide Deck:* [04_Invited Talk](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-iabopen-04-invited-talk-01)

* **Gap Analysis:** Highlighted the disconnect between technical authorization protocols (OAuth 2.0, OpenID Connect) and legal delegation mechanisms across 25 legal frameworks (CCPA, HIPAA, FERPA, tax, financial brokerage).
* **Identity and Agency Tussles:**
  * Analyzed active legal disputes (e.g., Amazon vs. Perplexity Comet, Meta Muse) stemming from AI agents impersonating users via browser cookies/credentials versus utilizing dedicated, verifiable agent credentials.
  * Under legal agency frameworks, an agent must be a legal person capable of contracting and assuming liability. Since AI models are not legal entities, legal accountability shifts to the platform/service provider.
* **Protocol Recommendations:**
  * Proposed incorporating verifiable references (such as URLs or cryptographic hashes) to underlying human-readable legal authorizations directly within technical OAuth scope/grant tokens (referencing PG&E's utility data sharing model).
  * Emphasized balancing accountability with privacy to prevent agent identity frameworks from becoming surveillance mechanisms on the open web.
* **Q&A Discussion:**
  * **Andrew Campling:** Inquired about incorporating jurisdictional metadata into delegation structures.
  * **Wendy Seltzer:** Suggested requiring counterparty verified credentials before financial transactions are authorized to prevent payment diversion attacks.
  * **Pete Resnick:** Noted that standard product liability frameworks apply to AI platforms, cautioning against conflating software products with legal agents.
  * **Wei Chuang:** Discussed privacy boundaries when user agents perform actions and highlighted domain vs. individual delegation scopes in email protocols.
  * **Tom Sato:** Noted an individual draft on agent accountability composition and related rules-as-code initiatives.

